--TEST--
Cooldown policy falls back to the package-supplied time when a Composer repository exempted via require-published-time provides no published-time, and says so.
--COMPOSER--
{
    "name": "acme/project",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "require": {
        "acme/library": "1.1.0"
    },
    "config": {
        "policy": {
            "cooldown": {
                "period": "1 week"
            }
        }
    },
    "repositories": [
        {
            "type": "composer",
            "url": "file://update-policy-cooldown-composer-repo",
            "require-published-time": false
        }
    ]
}
--RUN--
update -v

--EXPECT-EXIT-CODE--
2

--EXPECT-OUTPUT--
Loading composer repositories with package information
<warning>The publication date of 1 package version(s) from composer repo (%s) could not be verified, the cooldown policy relied on the time field set by the package authors (run with -vv to list them).</warning>
<warning>1 package version(s) withheld by the cooldown policy (run with -vv to list them).</warning>
Updating dependencies
Your requirements could not be resolved to an installable set of packages.

  Problem 1
    - Root composer.json requires acme/library 1.1.0 (exact version match: 1.1.0 or 1.1.0.0), found acme/library[1.1.0] but these were not loaded, because they are still in the cooldown period configured in "policy.cooldown" (available in %s, based on the package-supplied time field as the repository provides no published-time). To exempt the package from the cooldown policy, add it to the "policy.cooldown.ignore" config, or run the command with COMPOSER_POLICY_COOLDOWN_PERIOD=0 for a one-off bypass. To turn the feature off entirely, you can set "policy.cooldown.block" to false.

--EXPECT--
